summary:
Code review of all of phase 3 at c27afaf: the localized flash, package A (contact push, KO blink-off and respawn), package B (hold view, bend and knee) and the lead's fixes. I ran the full suite first: 457/457 pass in 31.7 s, with no SCRIPT ERROR or ERROR (captures/review_code/tests_full.log). I then ran seven probe batches of GUT scripts in my folder and recorded three combo_bot acts with Movie Maker.

The layer rules hold. The vanish path stays in presentation: the HUD bars drop on CombatEvents.vanished, debug draw skips gone bodies, and VFX, audio and rumble have no new writes. No simulation code reads a camera, and the new public API has ## docs. Presets missing the new keys load them from the defaults, and default.json matches TuningData (a test checks it).

These edge cases behave correctly:
- a body vanishing from under a pile (the top body drops to the floor within 15 ticks);
- a hidden rig keeps its ragdoll stepping;
- a knee KO while holding (the view eases out, the victim launches and is KO'd);
- a hit that breaks the hold, a release, and hold_max_frames;
- a reset during the blink-off;
- the player never blinks off;
- kicks with a zero-length lever or a contact inside the body, and the central kick with ragdoll_kick_at_contact off;
- the flash's next_pass is set and cleared exactly, and the flash lasts exactly hit_flash_frames.

Main defects:
1. HumanoidRig.flash_at() places the localized flash at the raw world contact point. A thrown pipe shows no flash. A flying-body impact shows at most a faint edge. A hit on a body that is already staggering flashes the wrong part: the uppercut on the hook-staggered dummy flashes the left shoulder, 0.36 m from the head it struck.
2. A dummy's respawn check ignores ghost bodies: lying, KO'd and held bodies, and a dummy that respawned the same tick. A dummy can blink back in standing inside a body lying on its post.
3. The first-person lens bobs 5-7 cm up and down on every grab and release. A release while walking also pops the lens up 3.5 cm in one frame. The hold view's rise and pull-back ease on their own timers, and the walking bob clamp switches on at once. The tests only check the offsets, not the lens.

Smaller findings: magnetism lunges at a dummy that is blinking in and can't be hit, and a thrown pipe bounces off it. dummy_revive_s changed meaning under the same key, so older presets now give 5.5 s from KO to back. Two tests have weak checks.

findings.detail:
The hold view's rise (grab_view_rise_m 0.10) and pull-back (0.24) ease on their own sim-tick timers: in over grab_view_in_s 0.2 s, out over grab_view_out_s 0.45 s (player.gd:924-928). The bend they make up for comes and goes with the pose blends in about 10 ticks, and the rise is larger than the bend's eye drop (0.083 m). Measured through the real FirstPersonCamera:
- standing release: the lens rises 4.7 cm above the normal view over 9 ticks, then sinks back over 10;
- grab while walking: the lens rises 6.4 cm (up to 1.2 cm a tick, against 0.38 when walking), then sinks;
- release while walking: first_person_camera.gd:170 turns the walking bob clamp on the instant holding_enemy() clears, so the lens jumps 3.5 cm in the first frame (0.79 cm worst when standing), climbs to 6.8 cm above, then sinks;
- horizontally the lens dips 3.5 cm back before moving 15 cm forward.
The values themselves are B's approved departures and an open question, but these bumps are a timing side effect. test_grab_view.gd only checks get_camera_move_offset() and get_camera_pull_back() (_offsets at :85, _hold_step at :109), never the lens path, so it passes with the bumps.
